{"title":"2001 IMO Problems/Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider an acute triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the foot of the altitude of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> issuing from the vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumcenter\" title=\"Circumcenter\">circumcenter</a> of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />. Assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/b/debb5c5cb6d79ca9e4fe56d8b3a585f0efabea6b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle C \\geq \\angle B+30^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"125\" height=\"15\" />. Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/b/ccbbad584a1e8483ed7bf6fc7bf0087ac0d12aae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A+\\angle COP &lt; 90^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"152\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> on the circumcircle with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/6/376e98a6571cc9c8a1e4ce1537be00715b24e7e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD \\parallel  BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"77\" height=\"19\" />. Notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/9/1e9feceb6fde0e38b29488efec480b21dac869b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CBD = \\angle BCA$\" width=\"137\" height=\"13\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/8/5e850dafb0e5d194810c09b7c6f17f4b24a9be26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABD \\ge 30^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"105\" height=\"15\" />. Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/3/7b39dbb06c042c741e52923b8eecc6da503a8454.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AOD \\ge 60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"104\" height=\"15\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> be the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/f/6afa6709014f5fe7ebf13fec2c3a6b554f57b591.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AZ \\ge R/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"18\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the radius of the circumcircle. But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/7/9b7738563e75b7967121f501fa6183d0694c78c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AZ = YP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"82\" height=\"14\" /> (since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/4/014a94f5c432752f97d318052bc47f1b8c2f8946.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AZYP$\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" /> is a rectangle).\n</p><p>Now <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> cannot coincide with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> (otherwise <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/1/7b1ac662f7ddbbce489953c8b7693848239564aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"14\" /> would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/e/22ec0d2e4202839322d26a23dcb13f79821df5d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> and the triangle would not be acute-angled). So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/8/d5869533621c110b4a131e02ffa55e04a7ac1890.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OP &gt; YP \\ge R/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"137\" height=\"18\" />. But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/1/011e0164a9fde46e293db0cefb5f7f346d156483.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PC = YC - YP &lt; R - YP \\le R/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"277\" height=\"18\" />. So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/b/8fb9ba92d8983b2ee6223ee799b4ddae8fa3ccae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OP &gt; PC$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"81\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/6/a36808fdfe4c80ebfdbf2d15ef9364c1ee1fba67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle COP &lt; \\angle OCP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"137\" height=\"15\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> be a diameter of the circle, so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/2/452db54aae253ad306ff5f152c618b611859f9e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle OCP = \\angle ECB$\" width=\"135\" height=\"12\" />. But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/4/364632997a3353d22aee1d1f81298333cc5f40e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ECB = \\angle EAB$\" width=\"135\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/8/d98cadeec2ef6d6d37a5450a871818b67b1125f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle EAB + \\angle BAC = \\angle EAC = 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"261\" height=\"14\" />, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/7/a771251838750a4cb9926c5e2b03324b2b2edae7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is a diameter. Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/3/89314a2aa14658bbf4475e85427c10915e02060c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle COP + \\angle BAC &lt; 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"181\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/a/1ca20ed7e2a746ff921f10369fb2d5000561ced9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{PCO} = 90^\\circ - \\angle{A}$\" width=\"154\" height=\"13\" />, it suffices to prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/1/5e1791037f44fee1e5d56db8b8f5301c0f20ddd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{POC} &lt; \\angle{PCO}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"135\" height=\"13\" />, or equivalently <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/3/9e36598f8a65f93f900e2a390c0015a6dfb233e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PC &lt; PO.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"85\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>Suppose on the contrary that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/a/7fa8d1c3e75da5b90baf9c83971e0e903ac9ffd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PC &gt; PO$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"80\" height=\"13\" />. By the triangle inequality, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26e269ebe5ce98b86fdcb19e6f972f70b5099e1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 PC = PC + PC &gt; PC + PO &gt; CO = R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"335\" height=\"14\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the circumradius of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />. But the Law of Sines and basic trigonometry gives us that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/3/2038bd69db1c5452f6c47957b29381b85a5124e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PC = 2R \\sin B \\cos C$\" width=\"163\" height=\"12\" />, so we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/6/fb61df1436ef7b81c7e1f190ba778278306bce2a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 \\sin B \\cos C &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"129\" height=\"13\" />. But we also have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/8/3c897e38689c258dc833775ce4eb9e6fc2b653b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 \\sin B \\cos C \\le 4 \\sin B \\cos (B + 30^\\circ) = 2 (\\sin (2B + 30^\\circ) - \\sin 30^\\circ) \\le 2 (1 - \\frac{1}{2}) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"631\" height=\"37\" /> because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/4/874d85553475acd470d89c01ae447903e38e4684.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{C} \\ge \\angle{B} + 30^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"125\" height=\"15\" />, and so we have a contradiction. Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/3/eb372374cf1a0f969daf93c37ce5de3260a0c3cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PC &lt; PO$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"80\" height=\"13\" /> and so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/6/756cd2ecf8036db977547c497f3a005b8c6d3d46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{PCO} + \\angle{A} &lt; 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"152\" height=\"14\" />, as desired.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/1/851888b6ac142797c883454dc15051e2fc31ca29.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(250);  // Settings for fonts and line styles defaultpen(fontsize(10pt)); pen thin_pen = black + 0.5pt; pen normal_pen = black + 0.4pt; pen heavy_pen = black + 1.2pt; pen dotted_pen = linetype(&quot;4 4&quot;) + black;  // Define points for acute triangle ABC with base BC on x-axis // angle B = 40 deg, angle C = 74 deg. c=8 for scaling. pair B = (0,0); pair C = (8,0); pair A = (6.4479, 5.4103);   // Draw triangle ABC path triangle_abc = A--B--C--cycle; draw(triangle_abc, thin_pen);  // Label vertices dot(&quot;$A$&quot;, A, N); dot(&quot;$B$&quot;, B, SW); dot(&quot;$C$&quot;, C, SE);  // Circumcenter O and Circumcircle pair O = (4, 1.7765); real R = length(O-B); // Radius path circumcircle = circle(O, R); draw(circumcircle, normal_pen);  // Mark circumcenter dot(&quot;$O$&quot;, O, NE);  // Altitude AP pair P = (A.x, 0); draw(A--P, dotted_pen); dot(&quot;$P$&quot;, P, S);  // Right angle mark at P (manually drawn without olympiad) real s = 0.3; // Size of the right angle mark draw((P.x+s, P.y) -- (P.x+s, P.y+s) -- (P.x, P.y+s), thin_pen);  // Line CO extended to intersect circle at D pair D = 2*O - C; path CO_ray = C--D; draw(CO_ray, dashed); dot(&quot;$D$&quot;, D, NW);  // Line segment AD draw(A--D, dashed);  // Line segment OP draw(O--P, normal_pen+dashed);  shipout(bbox(0.1cm, Fill(white))); [/asy]\" width=\"415\" height=\"412\" />\n</p><p>We extend <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/6/99647409e70660cdb500afd2c7da80fad46bb0e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{CO}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"21\" /> intersecting the circumcircle at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/c/abc50dff2910c5b365b37031b0440708030c830f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90^\\circ-\\angle BAC=\\angle BAD=\\angle BCD=\\angle OCP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"348\" height=\"16\" />, it is enough to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/1/0a1fc8505ec058590ea28c11c752d44004bb6126.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle COP&lt;\\angle OCP \\iff CP&lt;OP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"272\" height=\"15\" />. \n</p><p>We establish a complex coordinate with the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> being the unit circle, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/8/778b25c09f03bb6e1286f1f71bf754cd68a74eb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lvert a\\rvert=\\lvert b\\rvert=\\lvert c\\rvert=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"139\" height=\"20\" /> (let the lower case letter of each point denote its complex number). Thus,\n\\begin{align*}\nCP&amp;&lt;OP\\iff\\\\\n\\lvert c-p\\rvert^2-\\lvert p\\rvert^2&amp;&lt;0\\iff\\\\\n1-c\\overline{p}-\\overline{c}p&amp;&lt;0\\\\\n\\end{align*}\nOr <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/6/d46d8a7f7e5ae37be6385a0fb9373e90973032c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-2\\operatorname{Re}{(p\\overline{c})}&lt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"134\" height=\"20\" />. We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/a/99a21ce43e87afa11b1dce58aa1fc95fe9834337.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p=\\frac{1}{2}(a+b+c-\\overline{a}bc)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"180\" height=\"38\" />. It follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/1/0517d98e94aadd10cb1ed49d08ade129c5b93bbc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\overline{c}=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1+\\frac{a}{c}+\\frac{b}{c}-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"200\" height=\"45\" />. WLOG, we can rotate the coordinate plane so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/f/b2f77335d69e440ae4636364d01da0aad1282fe7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=1, a=e^{2iB}, b=e^{-2iA}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"197\" height=\"21\" />. Hence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/3/b5394e198cd6cccb5d8cbea76920e82949c7cdd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{a}{c}=e^{2iB}, \\frac{b}{c}=e^{-2iA}, \\frac{b}{a}=e^{-2i(A+B)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"274\" height=\"39\" />. By the Euler's Formula, the above inequality is equivalent to\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/a/f6af805938f92e31de43254e5fb027fef3f2218d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\cos{2A}+\\cos{2B}-\\cos{2C}&gt; 0\\]\" width=\"228\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>We have\n\\begin{align*}\n\\cos{2A}+\\cos{2B}-\\cos{2C}&amp;=2\\cos{(A+B)}\\cos{(A-B)}-2\\cos^2 C + 1\\\\\n&amp;=1-2\\cos C(\\cos{(A-B)}+\\cos{C})\\\\\n&amp;=1-2\\cos C(\\cos{(A-B)}-\\cos{(A+B)})\\\\\n&amp;=1-4\\sin A\\sin B\\cos C\n\\end{align*}\nAs given, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/8/b1882ea824404b8b6203b51263299d1f97019cf8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C\\ge B+30^\\circ\\implies B-C\\le -30^\\circ\\implies \\sin{(B-C)}\\le -\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"460\" height=\"38\" />. Substituting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/d/f6d049b29ce83bbd4d805ea1179c44df6f70b3f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sin B\\cos C=\\sin A +\\sin{(B-C)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"275\" height=\"20\" />, We see that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/4/2f4d7931adf50e1dc93a6904f3e091401d8ace32.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1-4\\sin A\\sin B\\cos C =1-2\\sin^2 A -2\\sin A\\sin{(B-C)}\\ge 1-2\\sin^2A+\\sin A=-(\\sin A -1)(2\\sin A +1)\\]\" width=\"786\" height=\"20\" />\nSince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is an acute angle, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/0/81029feba340db9c0143b186d5b95ab38462eaf0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-(\\sin A -1)(2\\sin A +1)&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"230\" height=\"20\" />, as desired.\n</p><p>~Pieandpi31415926535897932\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO\" title=\"2001 IMO\">2001 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Problems\" title=\"2001 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b>First question</b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2001 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2001 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2001 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2001 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2001 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2001 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017195332\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.032 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.046 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 369/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 867/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 52/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 13845/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.63